<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galle Fort</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> Fort is a fortified old town on the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The Portuguese built the first fortifications in the late 16th century, and after taking Galle in 1640 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rebuilt them on a much larger scale, with bastions and sea walls enclosing streets, churches and warehouses. It was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galle-fort.jpg" alt="Galle Fort" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rovin Shanila · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galle_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> was a busy port long before the Europeans came; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosmas-indicopleustes">Cosmas Indicopleustes</a> mentioned it as a port of call, and the Portuguese under Lourenço de Almeida made their first landing on the island there in 1505. In 1588, driven back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> by an attack of King Raja Singha I of Sitawaka, the Portuguese built a small fort of palm trees and mud, which they called Santa Cruz and later extended with a watch tower and three bastions. In 1640 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, allied with King Rajasinhe II, took the fort with some 2,500 men under Koster.</p><p>The British took over on 23 February 1796, a week after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> their southern headquarters. They filled in the moat, built houses and a lighthouse on the Utrecht Bastion, and in 1873 opened the Main Gate between the Moon and Sun Bastions. Galle's importance declined once the British developed Colombo as the main port in the mid-19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The fort stands on a small rocky peninsula and covers 52 hectares. Judging the Portuguese earth and palisade defences unsafe, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> encircled the whole peninsula with 13 more bastions of coral and granite; many walls date from 1663 and the sea wall was completed in 1729. The older gate, finished in 1669, still bears the emblem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> inside and the arms of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> outside. Within the walls is a grid of streets with low gabled Dutch colonial houses and street names of the colonial period, a sewer system flushed by the tides, the Groote Kerk, the great warehouse built around 1669 to store <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, the Meeran Jumma Mosque of 1904 and All Saints Anglican Church of 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fort withstood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxing-day">Boxing Day</a> tsunami of 2004, which damaged part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galle">Galle</a> town, and its buildings have been restored by the Galle Heritage Foundation with support from the Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. More than half of its inhabitants are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>. The warehouse houses the National Maritime Archaeology Museum, which studies the more than 21 historical shipwreck sites of Galle Harbour, and the Old Dutch Hospital reopened in 2014 as a dining and shopping complex.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galle Fort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
